{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfsYYh35Ydv3hUnHPtZxs","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I ruled R24F1 **FIX**: PowerShell's dot invocation lets a model run past the blackout hook. The verdict is committed as `b8d1cd78` in `docs/reviews/wi-834/025-ADJUDICATE-22cf09c.md`, with the `WI: WI-834` trailer.\n\n**What I reproduced:** `. claude -p x`, `. 'claude' -p x` and `$r = . claude -p x` are all allowed, while every `&` form is denied. The reader splits on `&` as an operator but keeps `.` as a word, which `_segment_command` then treats as the command. In a real PowerShell session, the dot form ran a native command, captured its output, and ran a quoted command name.\n\n**Where the coverage line falls:** the builder asked me to stop this class reopening one form per round, so the verdict draws a line. It follows the approved text: a model CLI \"as any command word\", with script wrappers not inspected. The test is which word PowerShell's or Bash's own grammar makes the command.\n- **Inside the coverage:** invocation operators. In PowerShell, `.` and `&` are grammar, not commands, so in `. claude` the command word is `claude`, exactly as in the already-denied `& claude`. Fixing this repairs a misreading.\n- **Outside the coverage:** wrappers that take a command as an argument, such as `Start-Process`, `iex`, `cmd /c`, `sh -c`, and Bash `exec`, `command`, `builtin`, `nohup` and `xargs`. There the command word is the wrapper. The approved text excludes these except for the two declared prefixes, `timeout` and `env`. Widening that would be an owner-level change, so future findings against wrapper forms should be dismissed by this line.\n\n**Cost:** about 5 lines in the PowerShell branch of the one shell-reading boundary, plus four TC-339 cases. A quoted `'.'` must stay data, and dot-sourcing a script must stay allowed. No re-attestation is owed unless TC-339's wording changes.\n\nMy probe script is i